/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/linux-plus.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("linux-plus", [["lx1",0,1,"A new USB network adapter is not working, and the admin wants to confirm whether its driver module is currently loaded in the running kernel. Which command shows this?",["modinfo","depmod","lsmod","insmod"],2,"lsmod lists the modules loaded right now (it reads /proc/modules). modinfo only describes a module file on disk, whether or not it is loaded.","1.0 System management: kernel modules",["modinfo describes a module file on disk (its parameters, dependencies and path) whether or not it is loaded; it does not report the running kernel's loaded modules.","depmod rebuilds the modules.dep dependency database; it does not list which modules are currently loaded.",null,"insmod inserts a single module file into the kernel; it loads a module rather than reporting what is already loaded."],1],["lx2",0,1,"On a UEFI server, which component loads the Linux kernel and the initramfs into memory during boot?",["systemd running as PID 1","The dracut utility","The /etc/fstab file","The GRUB2 boot loader"],3,"Firmware starts the boot loader (GRUB2 or shim + GRUB2), which loads the kernel and initramfs. systemd starts only after the kernel is running; dracut builds initramfs images but does not boot them.","1.0 System management: boot process",["systemd runs as PID 1 only after the kernel is already running, so it cannot be what loads the kernel and initramfs.","dracut builds initramfs images on disk ahead of time; it does not load them into memory during boot.","/etc/fstab lists filesystems to mount once the system is running; it plays no part in loading the kernel.",null],2],["lx3",0,1,"After adding a storage driver that must be available at boot on a RHEL-family server, the admin needs to rebuild the initial RAM disk. Which command does this?",["grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg","depmod -a","dracut -f","mkfs.ext4 /boot"],2,"dracut -f regenerates the initramfs for the running kernel. grub2-mkconfig rebuilds the boot menu, not the initramfs, so the new driver would still be missing early in boot.","1.0 System management: boot process",["grub2-mkconfig rebuilds the GRUB boot menu, not the initramfs, so the new storage driver would still be absent early in boot.","depmod -a rebuilds the module dependency map; it does not regenerate the initramfs image.",null,"mkfs.ext4 /boot formats the boot partition, erasing it, and has nothing to do with rebuilding the initramfs."],2],["lx4",0,1,"According to the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard, where should an application write its variable log files?",["/usr/log","/var/log","/etc/log","/opt/log"],1,"/var holds variable data such as logs, spools and caches. /etc is for configuration and /usr is meant to be read-only shared program data.","1.0 System management: filesystem hierarchy",["/usr is meant to be read-only shared program data and has no standard directory for variable logs.",null,"/etc holds configuration files, not variable log data, and there is no standard /etc/log.","/opt is for self-contained add-on software packages, not for system log files."],1],["lx5",0,1,"An admin wants to mount a new disk in /etc/fstab by UUID so the entry survives device name changes. Which command shows the partition's UUID?",["df -h /dev/sdc1","mount -a /dev/sdc1","blkid /dev/sdc1","fdisk -n /dev/sdc1"],2,"blkid prints the UUID, label and filesystem type of a block device (lsblk -f works too). df shows usage of mounted filesystems only and does not print UUIDs.","1.0 System management: storage",["df -h reports usage of mounted filesystems and does not print device UUIDs.","mount -a mounts all fstab entries; it is not a UUID query and this syntax is invalid.",null,"fdisk has no -n action; it edits partition tables and does not print UUIDs."],1],["lx6",0,1,"The /data logical volume in volume group vg_data is almost full. A new disk, /dev/sdc, has been attached. Which sequence adds the space to /data with the least disruption?",["vgcreate vg_new /dev/sdc; lvcreate; mkfs the new LV","fdisk to merge /dev/sdc into the LV, then run resize2fs","pvcreate /dev/sdc; vgextend vg_data /dev/sdc; lvextend -r","mdadm --create with /dev/sdc, then mount it over /data"],2,"Initialize the disk as a physical volume, add it to the existing volume group, then grow the LV; -r resizes the filesystem in the same step. Creating a new VG gives a separate volume instead of growing /data.","1.0 System management: LVM",["Creating a new volume group gives a separate volume rather than growing the existing /data logical volume.","fdisk cannot merge a disk into a logical volume, and resize2fs needs a larger LV to grow into first.",null,"mdadm --create builds a RAID array, and mounting it over /data hides the existing data instead of extending it."],3],["lx7",0,1,"An XFS filesystem on a logical volume must be made 20 GB smaller. What is the supported approach?",["Run xfs_growfs -D with a smaller block count","Unmount it and run resize2fs with the new size","Back it up, recreate it smaller, and restore","Run lvreduce -r on it while it stays mounted"],2,"XFS can grow but cannot shrink. The only way is back up, recreate a smaller filesystem and restore. resize2fs works on ext2/3/4, not XFS.","1.0 System management: filesystems",["xfs_growfs only grows an XFS filesystem; there is no supported way to shrink XFS with it.","resize2fs works on ext2/3/4 filesystems, not XFS.",null,"XFS cannot shrink, and running lvreduce on a mounted volume with a filesystem that can't shrink would corrupt data."],3],["lx8",0,1,"Running ls -li in a directory shows: \"1311 -rw-r--r-- 2 app app 812 data.txt\" and \"1311 -rw-r--r-- 2 app app 812 report.txt\". What is the relationship between the two files?",["report.txt is a symbolic link to data.txt","They are hard links to the same inode","They are copies made with cp -p","They live on two different filesystems"],1,"Both names share inode 1311 and the link count is 2, which is what a hard link looks like. A symbolic link would have its own inode, an l file type and an arrow to its target.","1.0 System management: files and links",["A symbolic link has its own separate inode and an 'l' file type with an arrow to its target, not a shared inode number.",null,"Copies made with cp -p would each have a different inode number; here both names share inode 1311.","A shared inode number means the files are on the same filesystem; hard links cannot span filesystems."],3],["lx9",0,1,"Which command creates a gzip-compressed archive of /etc that keeps file permissions?",["tar -czpf etc.tar.gz /etc","tar -xzpf etc.tar.gz /etc","tar -tzvf etc.tar.gz /etc","tar -cjpf etc.tar.gz /etc"],0,"-c creates, -z uses gzip, -p keeps permissions and -f names the archive. -x would extract and -j would compress with bzip2 despite the .gz name.","1.0 System management: backup and restore",[null,"-x extracts an archive rather than creating one, so it would not build etc.tar.gz.","-t only lists the contents of an archive; it does not create one.","-j compresses with bzip2, not gzip, which is inconsistent with the .gz name."],1],["lx10",0,1,"A web root must be mirrored nightly to host backup so files deleted from the source are also removed from the copy. Which command does this?",["scp -r /srv/web backup:/srv/web","cp -au /srv/web backup:/srv/web","rsync -av --delete /srv/web/ backup:/srv/web/","rsync -av --remove-source-files /srv/web/ backup:/srv/web/"],2,"rsync --delete removes files from the destination that no longer exist in the source, giving a true mirror. --remove-source-files deletes the source files after copying, which destroys the original.","1.0 System management: backup and restore",["scp -r copies recursively but never removes files deleted from the source, so it does not produce a true mirror.","cp cannot write to a remote host:path like that, and it has no delete-extraneous behavior.",null,"--remove-source-files deletes the source files after copying, destroying the original web root."],3],["lx11",0,1,"Before replacing a failing disk, an admin wants a block-level image of all of /dev/sdb saved to /backup. Which command does this?",["dd if=/backup/sdb.img of=/dev/sdb bs=4M","tar -cf /backup/sdb.img /dev/sdb","cp --sparse=always /dev/sdb1 /backup/","dd if=/dev/sdb of=/backup/sdb.img bs=4M"],3,"dd reads the input device (if=) and writes it to the output file (of=). Swapping if and of would overwrite the disk with the image, the opposite of what is needed.","1.0 System management: backup and restore",["This has if= and of= reversed, so it would overwrite /dev/sdb with the image file rather than back the disk up.","tar archives files from a filesystem; it cannot image a raw block device like /dev/sdb.","cp --sparse copies only one partition (sdb1) and is not a raw block-level imaging tool for the whole disk.",null],2],["lx12",0,1,"On a server managed by NetworkManager, which command sets a static IPv4 address that survives a reboot?",["ip addr add 10.0.0.5/24 dev eth0 && ip link set dev eth0 up","nmcli con mod eth0 ipv4.addresses 10.0.0.5/24 ipv4.method manual","ifconfig eth0 10.0.0.5 netmask 255.255.255.0","echo '10.0.0.5 eth0' >> /etc/hosts"],1,"nmcli connection modify saves the setting in the connection profile, so it persists. ip addr add changes only the running kernel state and is lost at reboot or when the connection restarts.","1.0 System management: network configuration",["ip addr add changes only the running kernel state and is lost at reboot or when the connection restarts.",null,"ifconfig is deprecated on NetworkManager systems and its changes are also non-persistent.","/etc/hosts maps names to IP addresses; it does not assign an address to an interface."],2],["lx13",0,1,"An admin needs to rename a server to web01 permanently. Which command should be used?",["hostname web01","hostnamectl set-hostname web01","echo web01 > /proc/sys/kernel/hostname","echo web01 >> /etc/hosts"],1,"hostnamectl set-hostname writes /etc/hostname and updates the running name. The hostname command and /proc change only the running value until the next reboot.","1.0 System management: network configuration",["The hostname command sets only the running name, which reverts at the next reboot.",null,"Writing to /proc/sys/kernel/hostname changes the runtime value only and does not persist.","Appending to /etc/hosts adds a name-to-IP mapping; it does not set the system hostname."],1],["lx14",0,1,"A server keeps resolving app.local through DNS even though /etc/hosts has a different entry for it. Which file controls the order in which these sources are checked?",["/etc/resolv.conf","/etc/nsswitch.conf","/etc/hosts.allow","/etc/sysconfig/network"],1,"The hosts: line in /etc/nsswitch.conf sets the lookup order (for example files dns). resolv.conf lists DNS servers and search domains but does not decide whether /etc/hosts is read first.","1.0 System management: network configuration",["/etc/resolv.conf lists DNS servers and search domains but does not control whether /etc/hosts is consulted first.",null,"/etc/hosts.allow is part of TCP wrappers access control and has nothing to do with name resolution order.","/etc/sysconfig/network holds settings like the hostname and gateway, not the lookup source order."],2],["lx15",0,1,"Which command sends both standard output and standard error of cmd to log.txt?",["cmd > log.txt 2>&1","cmd 2>&1 > log.txt","cmd | tee log.txt","cmd 2> log.txt"],0,"Redirections are processed left to right: stdout goes to the file, then stderr is pointed at the same place. With 2>&1 first, stderr is copied to the terminal before stdout moves, so errors still reach the screen.","1.0 System management: shell operations",[null,"With 2>&1 placed first, stderr is pointed at the terminal (the current stdout) before stdout is redirected, so errors do not land in the file.","tee writes stdout to the file and the screen but does not capture stderr at all.","2> log.txt redirects only standard error, leaving standard output going to the terminal."],1],["lx16",0,1,"What does cut -d: -f1,7 print for the line \"root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash\"?",["root /bin/bash","root:x:0:0","/root:/bin/bash","root:/bin/bash"],3,"cut splits on the colon delimiter and prints fields 1 and 7, joined with the same delimiter. Field 6 is /root, the home directory, not field 7.","1.0 System management: shell operations",["cut joins the selected fields with the delimiter (a colon here), not a space.","root:x:0:0 is fields 1 through 4, not fields 1 and 7.","/root:/bin/bash is fields 6 and 7; field 1 is root, not /root.",null],3],["lx17",0,1,"A shell variable APP_ENV=prod is set at the prompt, but a script started from that shell sees it as empty. What fixes this?",["set APP_ENV=prod","export APP_ENV=prod","local APP_ENV=prod","alias APP_ENV=prod"],1,"export marks the variable for the environment of child processes. Without it, the variable is shell-local; local only works inside functions and alias defines a command shortcut.","1.0 System management: shell operations",["Using set with an assignment does not export the variable; set manages shell options and positional parameters.",null,"local only works inside a function and still does not export a variable to child processes.","alias defines a command shortcut, not an environment variable."],1],["lx18",0,1,"On a KVM host, which command lists every defined virtual machine, including ones that are shut off?",["qemu-img info --all","virsh list --all","virt-install --list","lsmod | grep kvm"],1,"virsh list shows running domains; --all adds inactive ones. lsmod | grep kvm only confirms the KVM modules are loaded.","1.0 System management: virtualization",["qemu-img info reports on a disk image file, not on defined virtual machines, and has no --all VM listing.",null,"virt-install creates new VMs; it has no --list option for existing domains.","lsmod | grep kvm only confirms that the KVM kernel modules are loaded."],1],["lx19",0,1,"A VM disk image should grow only as data is written and should support internal snapshots. Which image format fits?",["raw","ISO 9660","tar.gz","qcow2"],3,"qcow2 (QEMU copy-on-write) is thin-provisioned and supports snapshots and backing files. Raw images are simple and fast but have no built-in snapshot support.","1.0 System management: virtualization",["A raw image is full-size with no thin provisioning and no built-in snapshot support.","ISO 9660 is a read-only optical disc filesystem format, not a growable writable VM disk.","tar.gz is a compressed archive format, not a bootable VM disk image.",null],2],["lx20",0,1,"Which command finds files under /var larger than 100 MB that were modified in the last 7 days?",["find /var -size -100M -mtime +7","find /var -size +100M -mtime -7","find /var -size 100M -atime 7","locate /var -size +100M -mtime -7"],1,"+100M means larger than 100 MiB and -mtime -7 means modified less than 7 days ago. Reversing the signs finds small, old files; locate searches a name database and has no size test.","1.0 System management: files and directories",["-size -100M finds files smaller than 100 MiB and -mtime +7 finds files modified more than 7 days ago, both the reverse of what is wanted.",null,"-size 100M matches files of exactly 100 MiB and -atime uses access time, not the size-over-100M / recently-modified criteria.","locate searches a filename database and has no -size or -mtime tests."],2],["lx21",0,1,"A USB backup disk listed in /etc/fstab is sometimes unplugged, and the server then drops to emergency mode at boot. Which mount option prevents this?",["noexec","defaults","nofail","sync"],2,"nofail tells systemd not to fail the boot if the device is missing. noexec only blocks running programs from the filesystem.","1.0 System management: storage",["noexec only blocks executing programs from the filesystem; it does not affect boot behavior when the device is missing.","defaults enables the standard options (rw, suid, dev, exec, auto, nouser, async) and still fails the boot if the device is absent.",null,"sync forces synchronous I/O to the filesystem and does nothing about a missing device at boot."],3],["lx22",0,2,"A new developer needs an account with a home directory and bash as the login shell. Which command creates it?",["usermod -m -s /bin/bash dev1","useradd -M -s /bin/bash dev1","useradd -m -s /bin/bash dev1","groupadd -m -s /bin/bash dev1"],2,"useradd -m creates the home directory and -s sets the shell. -M explicitly skips the home directory, and usermod changes an account that already exists.","2.0 Services and user management: local accounts",["usermod modifies an account that already exists; it will not create the new dev1 user.","useradd -M explicitly skips creating the home directory, which the developer needs.",null,"groupadd creates a group, not a user, and -m/-s are not valid groupadd options."],1],["lx23",0,2,"User alice is in several supplementary groups and must also be added to docker without losing the others. Which command is correct?",["usermod -G docker alice","usermod -aG docker alice","groupadd docker alice","chgrp docker alice"],1,"-a (append) with -G adds the group and keeps existing ones. -G alone replaces her whole supplementary group list with just docker.","2.0 Services and user management: local accounts",["usermod -G without -a replaces alice's entire supplementary group list with just docker, dropping her other groups.",null,"groupadd creates a group; it does not add an existing user to one.","chgrp changes the group ownership of a file, not a user's group membership."],1],["lx24",0,2,"Policy requires that alice change her password at least every 90 days. Which command enforces this?",["chage -E 90 alice","chage -M 90 alice","passwd -l alice","usermod -e 90 alice"],1,"chage -M sets the maximum password age in days. -E (and usermod -e) set an account expiry date, which disables the account rather than forcing a password change.","2.0 Services and user management: local accounts",["chage -E sets an account expiry date (here day 90 after the epoch), which disables the account rather than forcing a password change.",null,"passwd -l locks the account's password; it does not set a maximum password age.","usermod -e sets the account expiration date, not the password's maximum age."],1],["lx25",0,2,"The /etc/shadow entry for alice begins \"alice:!$6$Qw...\". What does the ! before the hash mean?",["The password has already expired","The account password is locked","The account has no password set","The user must change it at next login"],1,"passwd -l or usermod -L adds ! in front of the hash so no password can match. An expired password is shown by the aging fields, not by a ! prefix.","2.0 Services and user management: local accounts",["An expired password is indicated by the aging fields in /etc/shadow, not by a ! placed before the hash.",null,"A password that is not set shows as an empty field (::), not a ! in front of an existing hash.","Forcing a change at next login is done with a 0 in the last-change field (chage -d 0), not with a ! prefix."],2],["lx26",0,2,"Nginx has just been installed. It must start now and also start at every boot. Which single command does both?",["systemctl start nginx","systemctl unmask nginx","systemctl enable --now nginx","systemctl reload-or-restart nginx"],2,"enable --now creates the boot-time links and starts the unit immediately. start alone runs it now but it will not come back after a reboot.","2.0 Services and user management: systemd",["systemctl start runs the unit now but does not create the boot-time links, so it will not return after a reboot.","systemctl unmask only removes a mask; it neither starts nor enables the unit.",null,"reload-or-restart reloads or restarts a running unit and does not set it to start at boot."],1],["lx27",0,2,"systemctl status shows: \"Loaded: loaded (/usr/lib/systemd/system/cups.service; disabled; preset: enabled)\" and \"Active: active (running)\". What is true?",["It starts at boot but is stopped right now","It is running now but will not start at boot","It is masked and cannot be started at all","It failed and systemd is restarting it"],1,"disabled describes boot-time behavior and active (running) the current state. The preset only says what the distribution's default would be.","2.0 Services and user management: systemd",["This reverses the fields: disabled describes boot behavior (won't start at boot) and active (running) describes the current state (running now).",null,"The unit is disabled, not masked; a masked unit would show 'masked' and could not be active.","The Active line shows active (running), not a failed or auto-restarting state."],3],["lx28",0,2,"An admin must change a setting in a vendor-supplied systemd unit so the change survives package updates. What is the best method?",["Run systemctl edit to create a drop-in override","Edit the unit file in /usr/lib/systemd/system","Add the setting to the /etc/rc.local script","Copy the unit into /run/systemd and edit it"],0,"systemctl edit writes a drop-in under /etc/systemd/system/<unit>.d/ and reloads systemd. Files in /usr/lib/systemd/system are replaced by package updates, and /run is cleared at reboot.","2.0 Services and user management: systemd",[null,"Editing the file under /usr/lib/systemd/system works until the next package update replaces it, losing the change.","/etc/rc.local is a legacy boot script and is not the mechanism for overriding a systemd unit's settings.","Files in /run/systemd are cleared at every reboot, so the change would not persist."],3],["lx29",0,2,"In a systemd .timer unit, which directive schedules the job to run once a day at midnight?",["OnBootSec=daily","OnCalendar=daily","ExecStart=daily","WantedBy=daily"],1,"OnCalendar sets wall-clock schedules, and daily means 00:00 every day. OnBootSec takes a time span after boot, not a calendar expression.","2.0 Services and user management: scheduling",["OnBootSec takes a time span measured after boot, not a calendar keyword like daily.",null,"ExecStart belongs in the service unit and specifies the command to run, not a schedule.","WantedBy sets install-time dependencies (which target pulls the unit in), not a run schedule."],1],["lx30",0,2,"A crontab contains \"30 2 * * 1 /usr/local/bin/backup.sh\". When does the script run?",["At 01:30 every second day","At 02:30 on the 1st of every month","Every 30 minutes on Mondays after 2 a.m.","At 02:30 every Monday"],3,"The fields are minute, hour, day of month, month and day of week, so this is minute 30, hour 2, any date, any month, weekday 1 (Monday). The 1st of the month would go in the third field.","2.0 Services and user management: scheduling",["This misreads the fields: 30 2 is 02:30 and the trailing 1 is a weekday, not an every-other-day schedule.","The day-of-month field is *, and the trailing 1 is day-of-week (Monday), not the 1st of the month.","30 is minute 30 only, not an interval that runs every 30 minutes.",null],2],["lx31",0,2,"A runaway process must be asked to shut down cleanly so it can flush its buffers. Which signal should be sent first?",["SIGKILL (9)","SIGSTOP (19)","SIGCONT (18)","SIGTERM (15)"],3,"SIGTERM asks the process to exit and can be handled for cleanup; it is the default for kill. SIGKILL cannot be caught, so the process gets no chance to clean up. Use it only if SIGTERM fails.","2.0 Services and user management: processes",["SIGKILL cannot be caught, so the process gets no chance to flush its buffers; it should be used only if SIGTERM fails.","SIGSTOP only suspends the process; it does not tell it to shut down and clean up.","SIGCONT resumes a stopped process; it does not stop or terminate one.",null],1],["lx32",0,2,"A batch job with PID 4321 is slowing interactive users. Which command lowers its CPU priority without stopping it?",["renice -n -10 -p 4321","nice -n 10 4321","kill -STOP 4321","renice -n 10 -p 4321"],3,"A higher nice value means lower priority, so renice to 10 makes the job yield CPU. A negative value raises priority, and nice only starts new commands.","2.0 Services and user management: processes",["renice to a negative value raises the process's priority, making it use more CPU rather than less.","nice launches a new command with a priority; it cannot renice an existing PID, and 4321 is not a command.","kill -STOP freezes the process entirely instead of just lowering its CPU priority.",null],2],["lx33",0,2,"A long-running command was started in the foreground. How can it be moved to the background without restarting it?",["Press Ctrl+C, then run bg","Press Ctrl+Z, then run bg","Press Ctrl+D, then run fg","Run nohup %1 from the prompt"],1,"Ctrl+Z suspends the job and bg resumes it in the background. Ctrl+C sends SIGINT and usually ends the job, so there is nothing left to resume.","2.0 Services and user management: processes",["Ctrl+C sends SIGINT and usually terminates the foreground job, leaving nothing to move to the background.",null,"Ctrl+D signals end-of-input, not job suspension, so there is no stopped job to bring to the foreground.","nohup launches a new command immune to SIGHUP; it does not background an already-running job by job number."],2],["lx34",0,2,"On a RHEL-family server, the dig command is missing. Which command shows which package provides it?",["rpm -qa dig","dnf info dig","rpm -ql dig","dnf provides '*/bin/dig'"],3,"dnf provides searches repository metadata for the package that ships a file (bind-utils here). rpm -qa and rpm -ql query only packages that are already installed.","2.0 Services and user management: software management",["rpm -qa lists installed packages matching a name, but there is no installed package literally named dig.","dnf info dig looks up a package named dig, which does not exist (the file ships in bind-utils).","rpm -ql lists the files of an installed package named dig, which is not installed.",null],2],["lx35",0,2,"After adding a new APT repository on Ubuntu, which command should run before installing a package from it?",["apt upgrade","apt autoremove","apt update","dpkg --configure -a"],2,"apt update downloads the package lists, including the new repository's. Without it, APT does not know the new packages exist. apt upgrade installs newer versions of packages already on the system.","2.0 Services and user management: software management",["apt upgrade installs newer versions of already-known packages; it does not fetch the new repository's package list.","apt autoremove removes unused dependencies and has nothing to do with refreshing package lists.",null,"dpkg --configure -a finishes configuring half-installed packages; it does not download package lists."],1],["lx36",0,2,"An admin finds an unfamiliar /etc/ssh/sshd_config change and wants to know which installed RPM owns that file. Which command answers this?",["rpm -ql /etc/ssh/sshd_config","rpm -qi /etc/ssh/sshd_config","dnf search sshd_config","rpm -qf /etc/ssh/sshd_config"],3,"rpm -qf (query file) returns the owning package. rpm -ql lists the files in a package, so it expects a package name, not a file path.","2.0 Services and user management: software management",["rpm -ql lists the files contained in a package and expects a package name, not a file path.","rpm -qi shows package information and expects a package name, not a file path.","dnf search matches keywords in package names and summaries; it does not report which package owns a file.",null],2],["lx37",0,2,"A container runs nginx on port 80 inside the container. Users must reach it on host port 8080. Which podman run option does this?",["-p 80:8080","--expose 8080","--network host:8080","-p 8080:80"],3,"The format is -p hostPort:containerPort. -p 80:8080 would publish host port 80 to a container port that nothing listens on.","2.0 Services and user management: containers",["-p 80:8080 maps host port 80 to container port 8080, where nothing is listening.","--expose only documents that a port is used; it does not publish or map it to the host.","--network host:8080 is invalid syntax; host networking shares the host stack and takes no port mapping.",null],2],["lx38",0,2,"A database container loses its data whenever it is recreated from a new image version. What is the correct fix?",["Commit the running container to a new image every night","Keep the data in the container's writable layer","Raise the container's memory and CPU limits","Mount a volume for the data directory"],3,"Named volumes or bind mounts live outside the container's lifecycle, so the data survives when the container is replaced. The writable layer is deleted with the container.","2.0 Services and user management: containers",["Committing the container to an image nightly captures the image layers but does not reliably persist live data changes.","The container's writable layer is deleted whenever the container is removed, so the data is lost.","Raising CPU and memory limits does nothing to make the data survive a container replacement.",null],3],["lx39",0,2,"Which command shows log messages for the sshd unit from the current boot only?",["journalctl -u sshd -b","journalctl -k -u sshd","dmesg --unit sshd","systemctl list-units sshd"],0,"-u filters by unit and -b limits output to the current boot. -k shows kernel messages only, which would hide sshd's own entries.","2.0 Services and user management: logging",[null,"-k restricts output to kernel messages, which would hide sshd's own service log entries.","dmesg shows the kernel ring buffer and has no --unit filter for systemd units.","systemctl list-units shows unit states, not the log messages a unit has produced."],1],["lx40",0,3,"After running chmod 750 deploy.sh, what permission string does ls -l show for the file?",["-rwxr-xr-x","-rwxr-x---","-rw-r-x---","-rwx-w----"],1,"7 = rwx for the owner, 5 = r-x for the group, 0 = --- for others. -rwxr-xr-x would be 755.","3.0 Security: file permissions",["-rwxr-xr-x is 755, giving others r-x, but the final 0 means others get no permissions.",null,"-rw-r-x--- would make the owner 6 (rw-), but 7 means the owner has rwx.","-rwx-w---- would make the group 2 (-w-), but 5 means the group has r-x."],1],["lx41",0,3,"A user's umask is 027. What permissions does a new regular file created with touch receive?",["-rw-r--r--","-rwxr-x---","-rw-rw----","-rw-r-----"],3,"Regular files start at 666 and the umask removes 027, leaving 640. Directories start at 777 and would get 750; that is where the x bits come from.","3.0 Security: file permissions",["-rw-r--r-- is 644, the result of umask 022; a umask of 027 also removes read from others.","Regular files do not get execute bits by default, since the 666 base has no execute bits for the umask to remove.","-rw-rw---- is 660 (umask 007), but 027 also removes the write bit from the group.",null],3],["lx42",0,3,"ls -l /usr/bin/passwd shows -rwsr-xr-x root root. What does the s in the owner execute position mean?",["Only the owner can delete or rename it","It runs with the file owner's privileges","It is shared by every group on the system","It stays cached in memory after it exits"],1,"That is the SUID bit: the program runs with the owner's UID (root here), which lets passwd update /etc/shadow. Protection from deletion by other users is what the sticky bit does on directories.","3.0 Security: special permissions",["Restricting deletion or rename to the owner is the sticky bit on a directory, not the SUID bit.",null,"SUID has nothing to do with group sharing; group inheritance on directories is the SGID bit.","On modern Linux the sticky bit no longer keeps executables cached in memory."],1],["lx43",0,3,"Users share /srv/shared, and everyone must be able to create files but must not delete other users' files. Which command sets this up?",["chmod 1777 /srv/shared","chmod 2777 /srv/shared","chmod 4777 /srv/shared","chmod 0777 /srv/shared"],0,"The leading 1 sets the sticky bit, so only a file's owner (or root) can delete or rename it, as on /tmp. 2 is setgid, which controls group inheritance, not deletion.","3.0 Security: special permissions",[null,"A leading 2 is setgid, which controls group inheritance on new files, not who may delete them.","A leading 4 is setuid, which is meaningless for this deletion-protection purpose on a directory.","0777 sets no special bit, so any user could delete any other user's files."],1],["lx44",0,3,"Bob needs read access to report.pdf without changing its owner, group or other permissions. Which command grants it?",["setfacl -m u:bob:r report.pdf","getfacl -m u:bob:r report.pdf","chmod o+r report.pdf","chown bob report.pdf"],0,"setfacl -m adds an ACL entry for one user only. chmod o+r would let every user read the file, and getfacl only displays ACLs.","3.0 Security: access control lists",[null,"getfacl only displays existing ACLs, and -m is not one of its options.","chmod o+r grants read to every other user, not just to bob.","chown bob changes the file's owner, which alters more than just granting bob read access."],2],["lx45",0,3,"On an SELinux-enforcing server, files moved from a home directory into /var/www/html return 403 errors, although the permissions look fine. What is the correct fix?",["setenforce 0","restorecon -Rv /var/www/html","chmod -R 777 /var/www/html","semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 80"],1,"mv keeps the original user_home_t label; restorecon resets the files to the default httpd_sys_content_t context. setenforce 0 only hides the problem by turning off enforcement.","3.0 Security: SELinux",["setenforce 0 disables SELinux enforcement, which hides the problem rather than fixing the incorrect file context.",null,"chmod -R 777 changes standard permissions, but the denial comes from the SELinux context, not permissions.","semanage port adds a port type label; it has nothing to do with the files' context under /var/www/html."],3],["lx46",0,3,"Which command shows whether SELinux is currently Enforcing, Permissive or Disabled?",["aa-status","setsebool","ausearch","getenforce"],3,"getenforce prints the current mode (sestatus gives more detail). aa-status is the AppArmor equivalent and does not report SELinux.","3.0 Security: SELinux",["aa-status reports AppArmor's status, not the SELinux mode.","setsebool toggles SELinux booleans; it does not report the current mode.","ausearch searches the audit log for events; it does not print the current SELinux mode.",null],1],["lx47",0,3,"On Ubuntu, an admin wants an AppArmor profile to log violations without blocking them while it is being tuned. Which command does this?",["aa-enforce","aa-disable","setenforce 0","aa-complain"],3,"aa-complain puts the profile in complain mode: violations are logged but allowed. aa-disable unloads the profile completely, so nothing is logged.","3.0 Security: AppArmor",["aa-enforce puts the profile in enforce mode, which blocks violations instead of only logging them.","aa-disable unloads the profile entirely, so violations are neither logged nor enforced.","setenforce 0 is an SELinux command, not AppArmor, and it disables enforcement system-wide.",null],1],["lx48",0,3,"An admin ran firewall-cmd --add-service=https and HTTPS worked, but the rule disappeared after a reboot. What went wrong?",["firewalld does not support the https service","The rule was added without --permanent","The zone was set to trusted during boot","SELinux removed the rule at startup"],1,"Without --permanent, firewall-cmd changes only the runtime configuration. Add it with --permanent and then --reload (or use --runtime-to-permanent).","3.0 Security: firewalls",["firewalld does support the https service; it is a predefined service definition.",null,"A trusted zone would allow the traffic; it would not cause the rule to vanish after a reboot.","SELinux does not manage firewalld rules, so it did not remove the rule."],2],["lx49",0,3,"An admin connected over SSH is about to turn on ufw for the first time. What must be done first to avoid being locked out?",["Run ufw allow OpenSSH","Run ufw default allow incoming","Run ufw reset, then ufw disable","Run ufw deny 22/tcp for brute force"],0,"ufw's default policy denies incoming traffic, so SSH must be allowed before ufw enable. Allowing all incoming traffic by default defeats the purpose of the firewall.","3.0 Security: firewalls",[null,"Setting the default incoming policy to allow opens the host to all inbound traffic, defeating the firewall.","Resetting and disabling ufw turns the firewall off, leaving the host unprotected.","Denying 22/tcp blocks SSH, which would immediately lock out the remote admin."],2],["lx50",0,3,"Which sshd_config setting stops the root account from logging in directly over SSH?",["PasswordAuthentication yes","PermitRootLogin no","PermitEmptyPasswords no","UsePAM no"],1,"PermitRootLogin no blocks root logins, so admins sign in as themselves and use sudo. PermitEmptyPasswords no is good practice but does not stop root logging in with a password or key.","3.0 Security: SSH hardening",["PasswordAuthentication yes allows password logins in general but does not specifically stop the root account from logging in.",null,"PermitEmptyPasswords no blocks blank-password logins but still permits root to log in with a password or key.","UsePAM no disables PAM integration, which can break authentication features and does not block root logins."],1],["lx51",0,3,"An admin generated an Ed25519 key pair and wants to use key-based login to server01. Which command installs the public key on the server?",["ssh-keygen -R server01","ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519","scp ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 admin@server01:","ssh-copy-id admin@server01"],3,"ssh-copy-id appends the public key to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys with the right permissions. Copying id_ed25519 would expose the private key, which should never leave the client.","3.0 Security: SSH hardening",["ssh-keygen -R removes a host's entry from known_hosts; it has nothing to do with installing your key on the server.","ssh-add loads a private key into the local agent; it does not place anything on the remote server.","Copying id_ed25519 sends the private key, which must never leave the client; only the .pub file belongs on the server.",null],1],["lx52",0,3,"The ops group must be allowed to restart nginx with sudo, and nothing else. What is the safest way to add the rule?",["Append the rule to /etc/sudoers with echo","Add every member of ops to the wheel group","Create /etc/sudoers.d/ops with visudo -f","Edit /etc/sudoers directly in nano as root"],2,"visudo checks syntax before saving, so an error cannot break sudo, and a drop-in keeps the rule separate. Adding users to wheel grants full root, far more than needed.","3.0 Security: privilege escalation",["Appending with echo bypasses syntax checking, so a typo can break sudo for everyone.","Adding users to wheel grants full root privileges, far more than restarting nginx.",null,"Editing /etc/sudoers directly in nano skips validation, risking a syntax error that locks out sudo."],3],["lx53",0,3,"On RHEL 9, security policy requires locking an account after five failed login attempts. Which PAM module provides this?",["pam_faillock","pam_limits","pam_pwquality","pam_env"],0,"pam_faillock counts failed attempts and locks the account (settings in /etc/security/faillock.conf). pam_pwquality enforces password complexity, not lockout.","3.0 Security: authentication",[null,"pam_limits enforces resource limits (ulimits) such as open files and processes, not lockout after failed logins.","pam_pwquality enforces password complexity rules, not account lockout on failed attempts.","pam_env sets environment variables at login and has nothing to do with lockout."],1],["lx54",0,3,"An admin downloaded an ISO and a SHA256SUMS file from the vendor. Which command confirms the ISO matches the published checksum?",["gpg --symmetric SHA256SUMS","openssl rand -hex 32","sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS","md5sum --encrypt SHA256SUMS"],2,"sha256sum -c recomputes each hash and reports OK or FAILED. Verifying the GPG signature on SHA256SUMS (gpg --verify) proves who published it; --symmetric encrypts a file instead.","3.0 Security: cryptography",["gpg --symmetric encrypts a file with a passphrase; it does not verify a checksum.","openssl rand generates random bytes and has nothing to do with verifying a download.",null,"md5sum does not encrypt (there is no --encrypt), and it uses MD5 rather than SHA-256."],2],["lx55",0,3,"A laptop's data partition /dev/sdb1 must be encrypted at rest with a passphrase that is asked for at boot. Which command starts this setup?",["cryptsetup luksFormat /dev/sdb1","gpg --encrypt /dev/sdb1","chattr +i /dev/sdb1","mdadm --encrypt /dev/sdb1"],0,"cryptsetup luksFormat creates a LUKS header on the partition. Then luksOpen, mkfs and an /etc/crypttab entry finish the setup. chattr +i makes a file immutable but does not encrypt anything.","3.0 Security: cryptography",[null,"gpg --encrypt encrypts files, not a whole block device that must be unlocked at boot.","chattr +i makes a file immutable; it does not encrypt anything.","mdadm manages software RAID and has no --encrypt option."],2],["lx56",0,4,"Which statement best describes idempotence in an Ansible playbook?",["Every task runs on every host in parallel","Tasks run once and are then removed from the playbook","Each run rebuilds the host from a clean image","A second run changes nothing if the host already matches"],3,"Idempotent modules check the current state and act only when it differs, so reruns report ok instead of changed. Parallel execution across hosts is a separate feature (forks).","4.0 Automation: configuration management",["Running tasks on every host in parallel describes execution strategy (forks), not idempotence.","Playbooks are not consumed after running; they can be rerun at any time, so this is not idempotence.","Rebuilding a host from a clean image is reprovisioning, not the idempotent convergence of desired state.",null],3],["lx57",0,4,"Which command checks that Ansible can reach and log in to every host in the web inventory group?",["ansible web -m ping","ansible-playbook web -m ping","ansible-galaxy ping web","ansible-vault ping web"],0,"The ansible command runs an ad hoc module against a pattern; the ping module confirms SSH access and a usable Python. ansible-playbook expects a playbook file, not -m.","4.0 Automation: configuration management",[null,"ansible-playbook requires a playbook file to run and does not accept an ad hoc -m module argument.","ansible-galaxy manages roles and collections; it has no ping capability.","ansible-vault encrypts and decrypts secrets; it does not test host connectivity."],1],["lx58",0,4,"A playbook needs a database password, and the file will be committed to Git. Which tool should protect the secret?",["ansible-vault","ansible-doc","ansible-inventory","ansible-config"],0,"ansible-vault encrypts variable files or single strings with AES-256 and decrypts them at run time. The other tools display documentation, inventory or settings.","4.0 Automation: configuration management",[null,"ansible-doc displays module documentation; it does not encrypt secrets.","ansible-inventory displays and manages inventory; it does not protect secret values.","ansible-config views and manages Ansible settings; it does not encrypt secrets."],1],["lx59",0,4,"Before changing cloud infrastructure defined in OpenTofu, the team wants to see what would be created, changed or destroyed. Which command shows this without applying anything?",["tofu apply -auto-approve","tofu destroy","tofu plan","tofu fmt"],2,"plan compares the configuration with the state and prints the proposed changes. apply -auto-approve makes the changes without asking for confirmation.","4.0 Automation: infrastructure as code",["apply -auto-approve makes the changes immediately without pausing for review.","destroy tears down the managed resources rather than previewing proposed changes.",null,"fmt only reformats configuration files; it does not show what would change."],1],["lx60",0,4,"Which configuration management tool uses, by default, an agent on each node that pulls a compiled catalog from a central server?",["Puppet","Ansible","OpenTofu","Git"],0,"Puppet agents check in with the Puppet server and apply the catalog. Ansible is agentless and pushes changes over SSH.","4.0 Automation: configuration management",[null,"Ansible is agentless and pushes changes over SSH; it does not use a per-node agent pulling a catalog.","OpenTofu provisions infrastructure through state and providers; it is not a node agent applying a catalog.","Git is a version control system, not a configuration management agent."],1],["lx61",0,4,"Running ./backup.sh returns \"Permission denied\" even though the owner can read the file. What fixes this?",["chmod u+x backup.sh","chown root backup.sh","export PATH=backup.sh","source ~/.bashrc"],0,"The script needs execute permission to run as ./backup.sh. Running bash backup.sh would also work because bash only needs to read the file.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting",[null,"chown root changes the file's owner; it does not add the missing execute permission.","export PATH=backup.sh corrupts the PATH variable and does nothing to make the script executable.","source ~/.bashrc reloads shell configuration and is unrelated to the file's execute bit."],1],["lx62",0,4,"What does this print: grep -q root /etc/passwd; echo $?",["0","1","2","root"],0,"grep -q prints nothing and exits 0 when a match is found. It would be 1 for no match and 2 for an error such as a missing file.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting",[null,"An exit status of 1 is returned only when grep finds no match, but root does appear in /etc/passwd.","An exit status of 2 indicates an error such as a missing file, which is not the case here.","$? prints the numeric exit status, not the matched text, and -q suppresses grep's output entirely."],1],["lx63",0,4,"APP_PORT is not set. What does echo \"${APP_PORT:-8080}\" print?",["APP_PORT","-8080","An empty line","8080"],3,"${var:-default} uses the default when the variable is unset or empty and does not assign it. ${var:=default} would also assign it.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting",["The variable name itself is never printed; parameter expansion substitutes a value.","The :- operator is not part of the output; only the default value is printed, without a leading dash.","With :- and an unset variable, the default is substituted, so the line is not empty.",null],3],["lx64",0,4,"In a bash script, which test is true only when /opt/app exists and is a directory?",["[ -f /opt/app ]","[ -x /opt/app ]","[ -z /opt/app ]","[ -d /opt/app ]"],3,"-d tests for a directory. -f is true only for regular files, and -z tests whether a string is empty.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting",["-f is true only for regular files, so it would not confirm that /opt/app is a directory.","-x tests the executable bit, which does not confirm the path is a directory.","-z tests whether a string is empty and has nothing to do with file type.",null],1],["lx65",0,4,"What does this print: for i in {1..3}; do printf \"%s\" $i; done; echo",["1 2 3","{1..3}","123","3"],2,"Brace expansion produces 1 2 3, and printf \"%s\" adds no spaces or newlines, so the output is 123 followed by the newline from echo.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting",["printf \"%s\" prints values with no separators, so no spaces appear between the numbers.","Brace expansion does occur, producing 1 2 3, so {1..3} is not printed literally.",null,"The loop iterates over every value and prints all of them, not just the last."],3],["lx66",0,4,"A script runs curl ... | tee out.json and should stop if curl fails, but the pipeline always returns 0. Which option fixes this?",["set -x","set -u","set -o pipefail","set +e"],2,"By default a pipeline's exit status is the last command's (tee). pipefail returns the last non-zero status in the pipeline, so set -e can catch it. set -x only traces commands.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting",["set -x only traces commands as they run; it does not change a pipeline's exit status.","set -u makes unset variables an error and has no effect on the pipeline's exit status.",null,"set +e disables exit-on-error, which would ignore the failure rather than let it be caught."],3],["lx67",0,4,"What does this Python print: print(len({1, 2, 2, 3}))",["4","2","3","A TypeError"],2,"Curly braces with values create a set, which drops the duplicate 2, leaving three elements. A list [1, 2, 2, 3] would have length 4.","4.0 Automation: Python",["A length of 4 would be correct for a list [1, 2, 2, 3], but braces create a set that drops the duplicate.","The set retains 1, 2 and 3, which is three elements, not two.",null,"This is valid syntax and len() works on a set, so no TypeError is raised."],2],["lx68",0,4,"An AI assistant generated a shell script to delete old log files. What should the admin do before running it in production?",["Run it as root because AI output is pre-tested","Paste real credentials in to make it complete","Review it and test it on a non-production VM","Schedule it in cron before reading it through"],2,"Treat AI output like code from an unknown contributor: read it, test it somewhere safe and keep secrets out of prompts. A wrong path in a delete command can wipe data.","4.0 Automation: AI best practices",["AI output is not pre-tested, and running unreviewed code as root can cause serious, irreversible damage.","Real credentials should never be pasted into prompts or into untested scripts.",null,"Scheduling unreviewed deletion code in cron automates a potentially destructive mistake."],2],["lx69",0,4,"A bad commit was already pushed to a shared main branch. Which command undoes it without rewriting history for other developers?",["git reset --hard HEAD~1, then force-push","git checkout -- .","git stash","git revert <commit>"],3,"git revert adds a new commit that reverses the bad one, so everyone else's history stays valid. reset plus force-push rewrites shared history and breaks other people's clones.","4.0 Automation: Git",["reset --hard followed by a force-push rewrites shared history and breaks other developers' clones.","git checkout -- . discards uncommitted local changes; it does not undo a commit that was already pushed.","git stash temporarily shelves local changes; it does not revert a committed change.",null],3],["lx70",0,4,"Which command creates a new branch named feature/login and switches to it?",["git branch -d feature/login","git merge feature/login","git clone feature/login","git switch -c feature/login"],3,"git switch -c (or git checkout -b) creates the branch and moves HEAD to it. git branch -d deletes a branch.","4.0 Automation: Git",["git branch -d deletes an existing branch rather than creating and switching to a new one.","git merge integrates another branch into the current one; it does not create or switch branches.","git clone copies an entire remote repository; it is not used to create a local branch.",null],1],["lx71",0,5,"Writes to /srv fail with \"No space left on device\", but df -h shows /srv only 40% used. df -i shows IUse% at 100%. What is the cause?",["The disk was remounted read-only","The user's memory limit was reached","The filesystem has run out of inodes","The partition table is corrupted"],2,"Every file uses an inode, and millions of tiny files can use them all while blocks are still free. A read-only mount gives \"Read-only file system\", not \"No space left\".","5.0 Troubleshooting: storage",["A read-only remount produces 'Read-only file system' errors, not 'No space left on device'.","A user memory limit does not cause disk write failures like this.",null,"A corrupted partition table would cause mount or read failures, not IUse% reaching 100%."],3],["lx72",0,5,"df shows /var at 100%, but du -sh /var adds up to only 30% of the size. An old 20 GB log was deleted earlier. What is the most likely explanation?",["A running process still holds the deleted file open","du does not count files in hidden directories","The filesystem journal is using the space","The log was moved to swap by the kernel"],0,"Space is freed only when the last open handle closes. lsof +L1 shows deleted-but-open files; restart or signal the process to release the space. du does include hidden files.","5.0 Troubleshooting: storage",[null,"du does count files in hidden directories, so hidden files do not explain the gap.","The filesystem journal is small and would not account for a missing 20 GB.","Swap holds memory pages, not deleted disk files, so the log was not moved there."],3],["lx73",0,5,"uptime on a 2-CPU server shows load average: 8.10, 7.95, 7.80. What does this indicate?",["Far more tasks are runnable or waiting than CPUs","The CPUs are each about 8% busy on average","Eight users are logged in at the moment","The server has been up for about eight days"],0,"Load average counts tasks that are running, runnable or in uninterruptible (often I/O) wait. A sustained load of about 8 on 2 CPUs means heavy queuing. It is not a CPU percentage.","5.0 Troubleshooting: performance",[null,"Load average is a count of tasks in the run queue, not a CPU-busy percentage.","Load average does not count logged-in users.","The length of time the server has been up is uptime, which is separate from the load numbers."],2],["lx74",0,5,"A Java service keeps disappearing without writing an error to its own log. How can the admin confirm whether the kernel killed it for lack of memory?",["Check /etc/security/limits.conf for the service","Search the kernel log for OOM killer messages","Run free -h and look at the buff/cache column","Look for a core dump in the service's home"],1,"The OOM killer logs \"Out of memory: Killed process\" to the kernel ring buffer (journalctl -k or dmesg). free shows current memory use but not past kills.","5.0 Troubleshooting: performance",["/etc/security/limits.conf sets resource limits (ulimits); it does not record that a process was killed.",null,"free -h shows current memory use, not a past OOM-kill event.","The OOM killer does not leave a core dump by default, so its absence proves nothing."],3],["lx75",0,5,"top shows low user CPU but a high wa (I/O wait) value. Which tool best shows which disk is saturated?",["iostat -x","lscpu","nproc","uname -r"],0,"iostat -x reports per-device utilization, queue size and await times (from the sysstat package). lscpu and nproc only describe CPU hardware.","5.0 Troubleshooting: performance",[null,"lscpu describes CPU hardware and topology, not disk I/O activity.","nproc only prints the number of processing units available.","uname -r shows the running kernel version and is unrelated to I/O saturation."],1],["lx76",0,5,"A server can ping 8.8.8.8 but ping example.com fails with \"Temporary failure in name resolution\". What should be checked first?",["DNS settings in /etc/resolv.conf, tested with dig","The default gateway in the routing table","Whether the NIC driver module is loaded","Whether the firewall blocks outbound ICMP"],0,"Reaching an internet IP proves the NIC, gateway and ICMP all work. The failure is name resolution, so check the configured resolvers and test them with dig or resolvectl.","5.0 Troubleshooting: networking",[null,"Reaching 8.8.8.8 already proves the default gateway is working, so it is not the first thing to check.","The interface clearly works because IP traffic to 8.8.8.8 succeeds, so the NIC driver is not the issue.","ICMP to 8.8.8.8 succeeds, so the firewall is not blocking outbound ICMP."],2],["lx77",0,5,"A new service fails to start with \"address already in use\" on port 8080. Which command shows which process already listens on that port?",["ip route show","ss -tulpn","ping -c 4 localhost","traceroute localhost"],1,"ss -tulpn lists TCP/UDP listening sockets with numeric ports and the owning process. ip route shows routing, not sockets.","5.0 Troubleshooting: networking",["ip route show displays the routing table, not which process is listening on a socket.",null,"ping localhost tests reachability of the loopback, not which process owns a port.","traceroute localhost traces a network path; it does not list listening sockets."],1],["lx78",0,5,"A host can reach other machines on 192.168.10.0/24 but nothing beyond. ip route shows only \"192.168.10.0/24 dev eth0 proto kernel scope link src 192.168.10.20\". What is missing?",["A default route through the gateway","An IPv6 link-local address","A DNS server in /etc/hosts","A second address on eth0"],0,"With only the connected route, the kernel has no path to other networks. Add default via 192.168.10.1 (persistently with nmcli or netplan). DNS would not stop traffic sent to raw IP addresses.","5.0 Troubleshooting: networking",[null,"An IPv6 link-local address does not provide the missing IPv4 route to other networks.","Traffic sent to raw IP addresses does not need DNS, so a missing /etc/hosts entry would not block it.","A second address on eth0 would not create a path off the local subnet."],2],["lx79",0,5,"systemctl status app shows \"Main process exited, code=exited, status=203/EXEC\". What is the most likely cause?",["The unit is masked by the administrator","Another process already holds its port","The ExecStart program is missing or not executable","The service ran out of memory at startup"],2,"Status 203/EXEC means systemd could not execute the command in ExecStart (wrong path, no execute bit or a bad shebang). A masked unit refuses to start and never reaches exec.","5.0 Troubleshooting: services",["A masked unit refuses to start and never reaches the point of executing ExecStart, so it would not show 203/EXEC.","A port already in use produces a runtime bind error inside the program, not the 203/EXEC exec failure.",null,"Running out of memory at startup shows a different status or a kill signal, not 203/EXEC."],3],["lx80",0,5,"After a typo in /etc/fstab, the server boots into emergency mode with the root filesystem read-only. What is the correct recovery?",["Reinstall GRUB2 to the boot disk first","Run mkfs on the root filesystem","Remount / read-write and fix /etc/fstab","Boot to graphical.target and retry"],2,"mount -o remount,rw / makes the root filesystem writable so fstab can be corrected; then run systemctl daemon-reload and reboot. The boot loader is fine because the kernel already started.","5.0 Troubleshooting: boot",["The kernel has already booted, so the GRUB2 boot loader is fine and does not need reinstalling.","Running mkfs on the root filesystem would erase the entire system.",null,"Booting to graphical.target does not fix the fstab typo, which will still fail the mount."],3],["lx81",0,5,"A server boots to a graphical desktop, and the admin wants it to boot to a text console by default from now on. Which command does this?",["systemctl set-default multi-user.target","systemctl isolate multi-user.target","systemctl set-default rescue.target","systemctl enable graphical.target"],0,"set-default changes the default.target link used at boot. isolate switches targets now but does not change what happens at the next boot.","5.0 Troubleshooting: boot",[null,"systemctl isolate switches to the target now but does not change what happens at the next boot.","set-default rescue.target boots into single-user rescue mode, not a normal multi-user text console.","Enabling graphical.target keeps the system booting into the GUI, the opposite of the goal."],1],["lx82",0,5,"The kernel logs ext4 errors for the data partition /dev/sdb1, mounted on /data. What is the safe way to check and repair it?",["Run fsck /dev/sdb1 while it stays mounted","Run xfs_repair /dev/sdb1 to fix it","Run mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdb1 to clear errors","Unmount it, then run fsck /dev/sdb1"],3,"fsck (e2fsck) must run on an unmounted filesystem, or it can cause more corruption. xfs_repair is for XFS, and mkfs would erase the data.","5.0 Troubleshooting: storage",["Running fsck on a mounted filesystem can cause further corruption; it must be unmounted first.","xfs_repair is the tool for XFS filesystems, but this partition is ext4.","mkfs.ext4 reformats the partition, erasing all of its data.",null],3],["lx83",0,5,"A web app on an SELinux-enforcing host is denied access to a file even after chmod 777. Where should the admin look to confirm the cause?",["ausearch -m avc for AVC denials","The output of ls -l on the file","The web server's access_log file","The /etc/sudoers.d directory"],0,"SELinux denials are logged as AVC records in the audit log; ausearch -m avc -ts recent shows them. Standard permissions are already wide open, so ls -l will not explain the denial.","5.0 Troubleshooting: security",[null,"ls -l shows standard permissions, which are already wide open at 777, so it cannot explain the denial.","The web server's access_log records HTTP requests, not SELinux access denials.","/etc/sudoers.d concerns sudo privilege rules and is unrelated to SELinux file access."],2],["lx84",0,5,"ssh -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 fails with \"Permissions 0644 for '/home/ana/.ssh/id_ed25519' are too open\". What fixes it?",["chmod 644 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub","chmod 777 ~/.ssh","chown root ~/.ssh/id_ed25519","chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519"],3,"The SSH client refuses a private key other users can read. 600 limits it to the owner. The .pub file can stay readable because it is public.","5.0 Troubleshooting: security",["Changing permissions on the .pub file misses the point; the private key, not the public one, is too open.","chmod 777 ~/.ssh makes the directory world-writable, which weakens security and does not fix the key.","chown root on the key would make the user unable to read their own private key.",null],2],["lx85",0,5,"Kerberos and TLS logins on a server fail intermittently with clock-skew errors. Which command shows whether the host is synchronizing time and its current offset?",["hwclock --systohc","timedatectl set-timezone UTC","chronyc tracking","date +%s"],2,"chronyc tracking shows the reference source, stratum and system time offset. Changing the timezone does not fix skew, because UTC time is what matters to these protocols.","5.0 Troubleshooting: services",["hwclock --systohc writes the system time to the hardware clock; it does not show sync status or offset.","Setting the timezone to UTC does not change the underlying clock skew that these protocols care about.",null,"date +%s prints the current epoch time but reveals nothing about synchronization or offset."],3],["lx86",0,5,"Installing a .deb with dpkg -i fails because dependencies are missing, leaving the package half-configured. Which command resolves it?",["apt --fix-broken install","dpkg --purge --force-all","apt-mark hold","apt autoremove"],0,"apt --fix-broken install (apt -f install) downloads the missing dependencies and finishes configuring the package. Forced purges can leave the system inconsistent.","5.0 Troubleshooting: software",[null,"dpkg --purge --force-all forcibly removes packages and can leave the system in an inconsistent state.","apt-mark hold pins a package to its current version; it does not resolve missing dependencies.","apt autoremove removes unused packages; it does not install the missing dependencies."],1],["lx87",0,5,"Users report a slow server. Which command lists the processes using the most CPU, highest first?",["ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head","ps aux --sort=-%mem | head","free -m | head","lsof | head"],0,"--sort=-%cpu sorts by CPU usage in descending order. Sorting by %mem finds memory hogs instead, which does not match the symptom.","5.0 Troubleshooting: performance",[null,"--sort=-%mem sorts by memory use rather than CPU, so it does not match the reported symptom.","free -m shows overall memory totals, not per-process CPU usage.","lsof lists open files, not CPU consumption by process."],1],["lx88",0,5,"dmesg shows repeated I/O errors and read retries on /dev/sda. Which command reports the drive's own health counters, such as reallocated sectors?",["fdisk -l /dev/sda","smartctl -a /dev/sda","blkid /dev/sda","hdparm -z /dev/sda"],1,"smartctl (smartmontools) reads SMART attributes and self-test results from the drive. fdisk -l shows only the partition layout.","5.0 Troubleshooting: hardware",["fdisk -l shows only the partition layout, not the drive's SMART health counters.",null,"blkid reports the UUID and filesystem type, not SMART attributes.","hdparm -z re-reads the partition table; it does not report drive health."],1],["lx89",0,5,"A branch office can reach its own gateway but not app01 at headquarters. Which tool shows the hop where packets stop?",["arp -a","hostname -I","traceroute app01","ethtool eth0"],2,"traceroute (or tracepath / mtr) lists each router hop until replies stop, which shows where the path breaks. arp -a only shows local Layer 2 neighbors.","5.0 Troubleshooting: networking",["arp -a shows local Layer 2 neighbors on the same segment, not the remote path to headquarters.","hostname -I prints the host's own IP addresses and reveals nothing about the path.",null,"ethtool eth0 shows the local NIC's link settings, not where along the path packets stop."],2],["lx90",0,5,"podman run -p 8080:80 web fails with \"address already in use\". What is the cause?",["The image does not expose port 80 in its metadata","Rootless containers cannot publish any ports","Another process already listens on host port 8080","Port 80 inside the container is already taken"],2,"The host side of the mapping is what conflicts; each container has its own network namespace, so port 80 inside is free. Rootless podman can publish ports at 1024 and above.","5.0 Troubleshooting: containers",["Port publishing works regardless of whether the image's metadata exposes port 80.","Rootless podman can publish ports at 1024 and above, so this is not the cause.",null,"Each container has its own network namespace, so port 80 inside the container is free; the conflict is on the host side."],3],["lx91",0,5,"After a kernel update, a server fails to boot, but the previous kernel still works. What is the quickest way to get it running while investigating?",["Reinstall the OS from the installation media","Select the previous kernel from the GRUB menu","Run dracut -f from the firmware setup screen","Delete /boot and reboot with the old disk"],1,"GRUB keeps older kernels as menu entries. Boot the known-good one, then fix or remove the new kernel (and set the default with grubby or grub-set-default). Reinstalling is unnecessary.","5.0 Troubleshooting: boot",["Reinstalling the OS is unnecessary because the previous kernel still boots the system fine.",null,"The firmware setup screen cannot run dracut; rebuilding the initramfs requires a booted system.","Deleting /boot removes the kernels and boot files, making the system harder to recover, not easier."],2],["x1",0,1,"A Linux server will act as a router, so IPv4 forwarding must be enabled and must stay enabled after every reboot. What should the admin do?",["echo 1 > /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward","sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1","Set net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1 in /etc/sysctl.d/","Load the ip_forward module in /etc/modules-load.d/"],2,"Settings in a file under /etc/sysctl.d/ are applied at every boot (and immediately with sysctl --system). sysctl -w is tempting because it does enable forwarding, but only in the running kernel, so it is lost at reboot.","Domain 1: Kernel parameters",["Writing to /proc changes only the running kernel, so the setting is lost at reboot.","sysctl -w also changes only the running kernel and does not persist after reboot.",null,"IP forwarding is a kernel parameter, not a module, so modules-load.d does not set it."],2],["x2",0,1,"A workstation must never load the nouveau graphics module, because the vendor driver is used instead. Which change keeps nouveau from loading at boot?",["Run rmmod nouveau after each boot","Run modprobe -r nouveau once","Add nouveau to a file in /etc/modules-load.d/","blacklist nouveau in /etc/modprobe.d/"],3,"A blacklist line in a file under /etc/modprobe.d/ stops the module from being loaded automatically (rebuild the initramfs if the module is included there). modprobe -r is tempting, but it only unloads the module from the running kernel until the next boot.","Domain 1: Kernel modules",["rmmod after boot means nouveau still loads first and must be removed by hand every time.","modprobe -r unloads it only until the next boot; nothing stops it loading again.","modules-load.d lists modules to load at boot, which is the opposite of what is needed.",null],3],["x3",0,1,"An admin needs to see exactly which kernel command-line parameters the server used for the current boot. Which command shows this?",["cat /proc/cmdline","cat /etc/default/grub","cat /boot/grub2/grub.cfg","uname -a"],0,"/proc/cmdline shows the parameters the running kernel actually booted with. /etc/default/grub is tempting, but it holds settings for future GRUB configs and may not match what was used, for example if parameters were edited at the GRUB menu.","Domain 1: Boot process",[null,"/etc/default/grub holds settings for future configs and may not match the current boot.","grub.cfg lists menu entries, but the parameters could have been edited at the GRUB menu for this boot.","uname -a shows the kernel version and architecture, not the boot parameters."],1],["x4",0,1,"A team wants a Linux filesystem with built-in subvolumes and copy-on-write snapshots so they can snapshot before upgrades without using LVM. Which filesystem fits?",["ext4","Btrfs","XFS","tmpfs"],1,"Btrfs is a copy-on-write filesystem with native subvolumes and snapshots. XFS is tempting as a modern, scalable filesystem, but it has no built-in subvolume snapshots; snapshots would need LVM underneath.","Domain 1: Filesystems",["ext4 has no built-in subvolumes or snapshots; it would need LVM snapshots underneath.",null,"XFS lacks native subvolume snapshots, so it would also need LVM for snapshots.","tmpfs lives in RAM and loses its contents at reboot, so it cannot hold snapshots of the system."],1],["x5",0,1,"An admin ran lvextend -L +20G on /dev/vg_data/lv_data but forgot the -r option. The ext4 filesystem mounted on /data still shows the old size in df. Which command fixes this?",["xfs_growfs /data","mkfs.ext4 /dev/vg_data/lv_data","resize2fs /dev/vg_data/lv_data","pvresize /dev/sdb"],2,"resize2fs grows an ext4 filesystem to fill its now-larger logical volume, and it can do so while mounted. xfs_growfs is tempting because it also grows filesystems, but it only works on XFS, not ext4.","Domain 1: LVM and filesystems",["xfs_growfs only works on XFS filesystems, and this one is ext4.","mkfs creates a new empty filesystem, which would destroy the data on /data.",null,"pvresize grows a physical volume after its disk grows; the LV is already bigger, so the filesystem is what needs resizing."],2],["x6",0,1,"Two new empty disks, /dev/sdb and /dev/sdc, must be mirrored with Linux software RAID so the data survives one disk failing. Which command creates the array?",["mdadm --create /dev/md0 --level=0 --raid-devices=2 /dev/sdb /dev/sdc","mdadm --create /dev/md0 --level=linear --raid-devices=2 /dev/sdb /dev/sdc","mdadm --assemble /dev/md0 /dev/sdb /dev/sdc","mdadm --create /dev/md0 --level=1 --raid-devices=2 /dev/sdb /dev/sdc"],3,"RAID 1 mirrors data across both disks, so the array survives one disk failing. RAID 0 is tempting because it uses the same command, but it stripes data with no redundancy, so losing either disk loses everything.","Domain 1: Software RAID",["RAID 0 stripes data with no redundancy, so losing either disk loses all data.","Linear mode just joins the disks end to end and offers no redundancy.","--assemble starts an existing array; it cannot create a new one from empty disks.",null],3],["x7",0,1,"The /var filesystem is filling up. The admin wants to see which top-level directory under /var uses the most space, sorted by size. Which command fits best?",["du -sh /var/* | sort -h","df -h /var","ls -lS /var","lsblk /var"],0,"du -sh totals the space used by each directory, and sort -h orders the human-readable sizes. df -h is tempting, but it shows only the usage of the whole filesystem, not which directories use it.","Domain 1: Filesystems",[null,"df shows usage of the whole filesystem, not which directories consume it.","ls -lS sorts by the size of directory entries, not the total space inside each directory.","lsblk lists block devices and partitions, not directory usage."],2],["x8",0,1,"A file named report.pdf will not open in a PDF viewer. The admin wants to know what kind of data the file really contains, regardless of its extension. Which command should they use?",["stat report.pdf","file report.pdf","ls -l report.pdf","touch report.pdf"],1,"file inspects the content (magic bytes) and reports the actual data type, such as ASCII text or a gzip archive. stat is tempting because it reports details about the file, but only metadata like size, inode and timestamps, not the content type.","Domain 1: Files and directories",["stat shows metadata like size, inode and timestamps, not the type of content.",null,"ls -l shows permissions, owner and size, not what kind of data is inside.","touch only updates timestamps or creates an empty file; it reports nothing."],1],["x9",0,1,"A deployment needs /opt/app/current to point to the directory /opt/app/releases/v2, so it can be switched to a new release later. Which command creates it?",["ln /opt/app/releases/v2 /opt/app/current","cp -r /opt/app/releases/v2 /opt/app/current","ln -s /opt/app/releases/v2 /opt/app/current","mv /opt/app/current /opt/app/releases/v2"],2,"ln -s creates a symbolic link, which can point to a directory and be repointed later. A plain ln is tempting, but it creates a hard link, and hard links to directories are not allowed.","Domain 1: Files and directories",["Without -s, ln makes a hard link, and hard links to directories are not allowed.","cp -r makes a separate copy that will not follow later release changes.",null,"mv would move or rename the directory rather than create a link to it."],2],["x10",0,1,"Each line of access.log has space-separated fields. The admin wants to print only the 1st and 3rd fields of every line. Which command does this?",["cut -f1,3 access.log","sed 's/ /,/g' access.log","sort -k1,3 access.log","awk '{print $1, $3}' access.log"],3,"awk splits lines on whitespace by default, and print $1, $3 outputs the 1st and 3rd fields. cut -f1,3 is tempting, but cut uses tab as its default delimiter, so without -d ' ' it would not split on spaces.","Domain 1: Shell operations",["cut uses tab as its default delimiter, so without -d ' ' it will not split on spaces.","This sed replaces spaces with commas but still prints every field.","sort only reorders lines by key; it prints whole lines, not chosen fields.",null],2],["x11",0,1,"An admin wants to count how many requests each client IP (field 1) made in access.log and list the busiest IPs first. Which pipeline is correct?",["awk '{print $1}' access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn","awk '{print $1}' access.log | uniq -c | sort -rn","awk '{print $1}' access.log | sort -u | wc -l","awk '{print $1}' access.log | sort | uniq -d"],0,"uniq -c only counts adjacent duplicates, so the IPs must be sorted first; sort -rn then puts the highest counts on top. Skipping the first sort is tempting, but then the same IP appearing in separate places is counted several times.","Domain 1: Shell operations",[null,"Without sorting first, uniq -c counts only adjacent repeats, so the same IP is counted in pieces.","sort -u | wc -l gives the number of distinct IPs, not each IP's request count.","uniq -d prints each duplicated IP once without counts or ordering by volume."],3],["x12",0,1,"Every occurrence of http_port=80 in app.conf must be changed to http_port=8080, and the file itself must be updated. Which command does this?",["sed 's/http_port=80/http_port=8080/g' app.conf","sed -i 's/http_port=80/http_port=8080/g' app.conf","grep -i 'http_port=8080' app.conf","tr 'http_port=80' 'http_port=8080' < app.conf > app.conf"],1,"sed -i edits the file in place, and the s///g expression replaces every match. The same sed without -i is tempting, but it only prints the changed text to the screen and leaves the file unchanged.","Domain 1: Shell operations",["Without -i, sed prints the edited text to the screen and leaves app.conf unchanged.",null,"grep only searches for text; it cannot change the file.","tr maps single characters, not strings, and redirecting to the same file truncates it first."],3],["x13",0,1,"An admin edited a YAML file in /etc/netplan on a remote Ubuntu server. They want to apply it but have it roll back automatically if they lose connectivity and cannot confirm. Which command should they use?",["netplan generate","systemctl restart systemd-resolved","netplan try","nmcli connection reload"],2,"netplan try applies the configuration and reverts it after a timeout unless the admin confirms. netplan generate is tempting because it processes the YAML, but it only writes backend config and does not apply it or offer a rollback.","Domain 1: Network configuration",["netplan generate only writes backend config; it neither applies it nor offers a rollback.","Restarting systemd-resolved affects DNS resolution, not netplan's interface configuration.",null,"nmcli connection reload rereads NetworkManager profiles and has no automatic rollback."],2],["x14",0,1,"An admin needs an archive of /var/log/app compressed with xz. Which command creates it?",["tar -czf logs.tar.xz /var/log/app","tar -cjf logs.tar.xz /var/log/app","tar -xJf logs.tar.xz /var/log/app","tar -cJf logs.tar.xz /var/log/app"],3,"In GNU tar, -c creates an archive and -J selects xz compression. -czf is tempting because it is the most common form, but -z means gzip; naming the file .xz does not change the compression.","Domain 1: Backup and restore",["-z selects gzip; naming the file .xz does not make it xz-compressed.","-j selects bzip2 compression, not xz.","-x extracts an archive rather than creating one.",null],1],["x15",0,2,"Every new user account should start with a company-standard .bashrc that includes common aliases. Where should the admin place this file?",["/etc/skel","/etc/profile.d","/etc/login.defs","/root"],0,"useradd -m copies the contents of /etc/skel into each new home directory. /etc/profile.d is tempting because it customizes shells, but its scripts run for all login shells system-wide and are not copied into new home directories.","Domain 2: Account files",[null,"Scripts in /etc/profile.d run for all login shells and are not copied into new home directories.","/etc/login.defs sets defaults like UID ranges and password aging, not starter dotfiles.","/root is root's home directory, so its files are not given to new users."],2],["x16",0,2,"An employee has left. Their account, home directory and mail spool must all be removed from the server. Which command does this?",["usermod -L jdoe","userdel -r jdoe","userdel jdoe","passwd -d jdoe"],1,"userdel -r deletes the account together with its home directory and mail spool. Plain userdel is tempting, but it removes only the account and leaves the home directory and files behind.","Domain 2: Local accounts",["usermod -L only locks the password; the account and files remain.",null,"Plain userdel removes the account but leaves the home directory and mail spool.","passwd -d removes the password, which weakens the account instead of deleting it."],1],["x17",0,2,"A security review requires that rpcbind.service can't be started at all, whether manually or when another unit pulls it in as a dependency. Which command meets this?",["systemctl disable rpcbind.service","systemctl stop rpcbind.service","systemctl mask rpcbind.service","systemctl daemon-reload"],2,"Masking links the unit to /dev/null so it cannot be started by anyone or anything until unmasked. disable is tempting, but it only removes boot-time start; the service can still be started manually or as a dependency.","Domain 2: systemd units",["disable stops boot-time start, but the unit can still be started manually or as a dependency.","stop halts it now, but it can start again at boot or when needed.",null,"daemon-reload rereads unit files and does not block any service."],2],["x18",0,2,"A maintenance script must run exactly once, at 23:00 tonight, and never again. Which approach fits best?",["Add 0 23 * * * to the crontab","Run systemctl start --at 23:00 job.service","Run nohup /usr/local/bin/job.sh 23:00 &","Pipe /usr/local/bin/job.sh into at 23:00"],3,"The at command schedules a one-time job at the given time. A crontab entry is tempting because it runs at 23:00, but 0 23 * * * repeats every day rather than once.","Domain 2: Scheduling",["This cron entry runs every day at 23:00, not just once.","systemctl start has no --at option for scheduling a later start.","nohup keeps a job running after logout; it does not delay it until 23:00.",null],2],["x19",0,2,"An admin connected over SSH must start a long import job that has to keep running after they log out, and no terminal multiplexer is installed. Which command fits?",["nohup ./import.sh &","./import.sh &","bg ./import.sh","fg ./import.sh"],0,"nohup makes the command ignore the hangup signal sent when the session ends, and & runs it in the background. Just adding & is tempting, but a plain background job can still get SIGHUP and stop when the SSH session closes.","Domain 2: Processes and jobs",[null,"A plain background job can still receive SIGHUP and stop when the SSH session closes.","bg resumes an existing stopped job; it does not start a new command.","fg brings a job to the foreground, where it would stop when the session ends."],2],["x20",0,2,"An admin wants only error-level and more severe messages from the nginx unit, starting from yesterday. Which command is correct?",["journalctl -u nginx --since yesterday | grep err","journalctl -u nginx -p err --since yesterday","journalctl -k -p err --since yesterday","journalctl -u nginx -b -1 -p err"],1,"-u picks the unit, -p err shows priority err and higher (crit, alert, emerg), and --since limits the time. Piping to grep err is tempting, but it matches text, missing critical messages that lack the word and including harmless lines that contain it.","Domain 2: Logging",["grep matches text, missing critical messages without the word and catching harmless lines that contain it.",null,"-k shows kernel messages only, not the nginx unit's logs.","-b -1 shows the previous boot only, not everything since yesterday."],3],["x21",0,2,"An application writes to /var/log/myapp/app.log, which grows forever. It should be rotated weekly with four compressed copies kept. What should the admin do?",["Add a weekly cron job that deletes the log file","Set SystemMaxUse in journald.conf","Create a file in /etc/logrotate.d/ for the log","Add the log path to /etc/rsyslog.conf"],2,"A logrotate config in /etc/logrotate.d/ with weekly, rotate 4 and compress handles this. journald's SystemMaxUse is tempting because it limits log size, but it applies only to the systemd journal, not to an app's own log files.","Domain 2: Logging",["Deleting the log loses all history and does not keep four compressed copies.","SystemMaxUse limits the systemd journal only, not an app's own log files.",null,"rsyslog routes messages to files; it does not rotate or compress them."],2],["x22",0,2,"All syslog messages must be forwarded from a server to the central log host 10.0.0.50 over UDP port 514. Which rsyslog change does this?",["Add *.* @@10.0.0.50:514 to the rsyslog config","Add 10.0.0.50 to logrotate's postrotate script","Run journalctl --forward-to 10.0.0.50:514","Add *.* @10.0.0.50:514 to the rsyslog config"],3,"In rsyslog, a single @ forwards over UDP and *.* selects every facility and priority. The @@ rule is tempting because it looks almost identical, but a double @@ forwards over TCP, not UDP.","Domain 2: Logging",["A double @@ forwards over TCP, not UDP as required.","logrotate's postrotate runs after rotation; it does not forward messages.","journalctl only reads the journal; it has no option to forward to a syslog host.",null],3],["x23",0,2,"On an Ubuntu server, an admin wants to know which installed package provided the file /usr/bin/curl. Which command answers this?",["dpkg -S /usr/bin/curl","dpkg -L /usr/bin/curl","apt show /usr/bin/curl","apt-mark showmanual curl"],0,"dpkg -S searches installed packages for the one that owns a given file. dpkg -L is tempting because it is also a file query, but it works the other way, listing the files that belong to a named package.","Domain 2: Package management",[null,"dpkg -L lists the files in a named package; it does not find a file's owner.","apt show describes a package by name and does not take a file path.","apt-mark showmanual lists manually installed packages, not file ownership."],1],["x24",0,2,"A user needs a newer version of a desktop app than the distro repo offers. It should run sandboxed with its own bundled dependencies and without touching system libraries. What should the admin use?",["Build it from source with make and install into /usr","Install it as a Flatpak from a trusted remote","Download an .rpm built for another distribution","Run pip install for it system-wide"],1,"Flatpak apps bundle their runtimes, run sandboxed and are installed separately from system libraries. Building from source is tempting to get the newest version, but installing into /usr can overwrite or conflict with system files and gives no sandbox.","Domain 2: Sandboxed packages",["Installing into /usr from source can conflict with distro files and provides no sandbox.",null,"Packages built for another distro often have mismatched dependencies and may break.","pip installs Python packages; it does not install sandboxed desktop apps."],2],["x25",0,2,"A podman container named api exits a few seconds after it starts. The admin wants to see what the application printed before it stopped. Which command should they run?",["podman ps -a","podman images","podman logs api","podman pull api"],2,"podman logs shows the stdout and stderr the container produced, even after it has exited. podman ps -a is tempting because it shows the container exited, but it gives only status and exit code, not the application's messages.","Domain 2: Containers",["ps -a shows that the container exited and its exit code, not what the app printed.","podman images lists local images, not container output.",null,"pull downloads an image; it does not show the container's output."],1],["x26",0,2,"An app runs in Kubernetes as pods managed by a Deployment. The pods get new IP addresses whenever they are recreated, but other apps need one stable address to reach them. What should be created?",["A second Deployment","A ReplicaSet","A ConfigMap","A Service"],3,"A Service gives a stable virtual IP and DNS name that load-balances to the matching pods. A ReplicaSet is tempting because it manages pods, but it only keeps a number of pods running and provides no stable network address.","Domain 2: Container orchestration",["Another Deployment creates more pods with changing IPs; it gives no stable address.","A ReplicaSet keeps a set number of pods running but provides no network address.","A ConfigMap stores configuration data, not network access to pods.",null],2],["x27",0,3,"The directory /srv/proj is owned by group projteam. Every new file created in it should automatically belong to group projteam, whoever creates it. Which command does this?",["chmod g+s /srv/proj","chmod +t /srv/proj","chmod u+s /srv/proj","chown :projteam /srv/proj/*"],0,"SGID on a directory makes new files inherit the directory's group. The chown option is tempting because it sets the group, but it changes only files that exist now, not ones created later.","Domain 3: Special permissions",[null,"The sticky bit limits who can delete files; it does not set group ownership.","SUID on a directory is ignored on Linux and does not set group ownership.","chown changes only existing files, not files created later."],2],["x28",0,3,"Web content lives in /srv/web on an SELinux-enforcing server. chcon -t httpd_sys_content_t fixed access, but a later restorecon changed the labels back and the 403 errors returned. What is the lasting fix?",["chcon -R -t httpd_sys_content_t /srv/web","semanage fcontext -a -t httpd_sys_content_t '/srv/web(/.*)?' then restorecon -Rv /srv/web","setsebool -P httpd_enable_homedirs on","setenforce 0"],1,"semanage fcontext adds a persistent labeling rule to the policy, and restorecon then applies it, so relabels keep the right type. chcon is tempting because it works immediately, but it only changes the current label, which restorecon or a relabel reverts.","Domain 3: SELinux",["chcon changes only the current label, which restorecon or a relabel reverts.",null,"This boolean covers home directory content, not files in /srv/web.","Disabling enforcement removes SELinux protection instead of fixing the label."],3],["x29",0,3,"On an SELinux-enforcing server, the Apache web app can't connect to a remote PostgreSQL database. ausearch shows AVC denials, and the admin finds a matching boolean. Which command allows the connection permanently?",["setenforce 0","chcon -t httpd_t /var/www/html","setsebool -P httpd_can_network_connect_db on","semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 5432"],2,"The httpd_can_network_connect_db boolean lets httpd connect to database ports, and -P makes it persist across reboots. semanage port is tempting, but labeling 5432 as http_port_t concerns which ports httpd may listen on, not outbound database connections.","Domain 3: SELinux",["setenforce 0 turns off SELinux enforcement instead of allowing just this access.","httpd_t is a process domain; putting it on files does not allow database connections.",null,"Labeling 5432 as http_port_t concerns ports httpd may listen on, not outbound connections."],3],["x30",0,3,"With firewalld, all traffic from the management subnet 10.1.1.0/24 should be handled by the internal zone. Which firewall-cmd options bind that subnet to the zone?",["--zone=internal --add-interface=10.1.1.0/24","--zone=internal --add-service=10.1.1.0/24","--zone=internal --add-port=10.1.1.0/24","--zone=internal --add-source=10.1.1.0/24"],3,"--add-source assigns traffic from an address or subnet to a zone, whose rules then apply. --add-interface is tempting because it also binds traffic to a zone, but it takes a network interface name like eth0, not a subnet.","Domain 3: Firewalls",["--add-interface takes an interface name like eth0, not a subnet.","--add-service takes a service name such as ssh, not an address range.","--add-port takes a port and protocol such as 22/tcp, not a subnet.",null],2],["x31",0,3,"All admins now log in to a server with SSH keys. Password logins over SSH must be turned off. What should be changed?",["Set PasswordAuthentication no and reload sshd","Set PermitRootLogin no and reload sshd","Set PubkeyAuthentication no and reload sshd","Delete ~/.ssh/authorized_keys and reload sshd"],0,"PasswordAuthentication no makes sshd refuse password logins while keys keep working. PermitRootLogin no is tempting as a hardening step, but it only blocks root, and other users could still log in with passwords.","Domain 3: SSH hardening",[null,"PermitRootLogin no blocks only root; other users could still use passwords.","Disabling public keys would block key logins, the opposite of the goal.","Deleting authorized_keys breaks key logins while leaving passwords enabled."],2],["x32",0,3,"A junior admin wants to see exactly which commands their account is allowed to run with sudo on a server. Which command shows this?",["sudo -i","sudo -l","su -l","visudo -c"],1,"sudo -l lists the sudo rules that apply to the current user. visudo -c is tempting because it relates to sudoers, but it only checks the sudoers files for syntax errors and needs root.","Domain 3: Privilege escalation",["sudo -i opens a root shell; it does not list the allowed commands.",null,"su -l switches to another user's login shell and says nothing about sudo rules.","visudo -c only checks the sudoers files for syntax errors."],1],["x33",0,3,"Policy requires new passwords to be at least 14 characters and to include several character classes. Which PAM module enforces this?",["pam_faillock","pam_limits","pam_pwquality","pam_sss"],2,"pam_pwquality checks new passwords for length, character classes and other complexity rules. pam_faillock is tempting because it is also an authentication policy, but it locks accounts after failed logins and does not check password strength.","Domain 3: Authentication",["pam_faillock locks accounts after failed logins; it does not check password strength.","pam_limits sets resource limits such as open files, not password rules.",null,"pam_sss connects PAM to SSSD for directory logins; it does not enforce complexity."],1],["x34",0,3,"Linux servers must let users sign in with their existing directory (LDAP or Active Directory) accounts and cache credentials so logins work if the directory is briefly unreachable. Which service provides this?",["nscd","chronyd","rsyslog","SSSD"],3,"SSSD connects Linux to LDAP, Active Directory and Kerberos identity sources and can cache credentials for offline logins. nscd is tempting because it caches name service lookups, but it does not authenticate users against a directory.","Domain 3: Authentication",["nscd caches name service lookups but does not authenticate users against a directory.","chronyd synchronizes time; it provides no identity or login service.","rsyslog handles log messages, not user authentication.",null],1],["x35",0,3,"An admin has downloaded an ISO, a SHA256SUMS file and its detached signature SHA256SUMS.sig, and has imported the vendor's public key. What proves that SHA256SUMS really came from the vendor?",["gpg --verify SHA256SUMS.sig SHA256SUMS","sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS","md5sum SHA256SUMS","openssl rand -hex 32"],0,"gpg --verify checks the detached signature with the vendor's public key, proving who published the checksum file. sha256sum -c is tempting, but it only compares the ISO with the checksums; a tampered checksum file would still pass.","Domain 3: Cryptography",[null,"sha256sum -c checks the ISO against the file but cannot prove who wrote the file.","An MD5 hash of the checksum file proves nothing about who published it.","openssl rand generates random data and verifies nothing."],3],["x36",0,3,"A security team wants to find unauthorized changes to binaries in /usr/bin by comparing their current hashes and attributes with a stored baseline database. Which tool is built for this?",["auditd","AIDE","fail2ban","SSSD"],1,"AIDE builds a database of file hashes and attributes and reports differences on later checks. auditd is tempting because it is also a security tool, but it logs events as they happen rather than comparing files against a baseline.","Domain 3: OS hardening",["auditd logs events as they happen rather than comparing files with a hash baseline.",null,"fail2ban bans IPs after failed logins; it does not check file integrity.","SSSD provides directory authentication, not file integrity checking."],1],["x37",0,3,"Auditors want every write to /etc/sudoers and every change to its attributes recorded, along with who made it. Which command sets this up?",["chattr +i /etc/sudoers","aide --check","auditctl -w /etc/sudoers -p wa -k sudoers","getfacl /etc/sudoers"],2,"An auditd watch with -p wa logs writes and attribute changes, and the key makes events easy to find with ausearch -k. chattr +i is tempting for protecting the file, but it blocks changes instead of recording who made them.","Domain 3: Compliance and auditing",["chattr +i blocks changes instead of recording who made them.","aide --check compares files with a baseline later; it does not log who changed them.",null,"getfacl shows the file's ACLs; it does not record any changes."],2],["x38",0,4,"A script runs correctly with bash deploy.sh, but ./deploy.sh fails with syntax errors on its array lines. The first line is #!/bin/sh. What fixes this?",["Run chmod +x deploy.sh","Add set -e near the top","Rename the file to deploy.bash and run it again","Change the first line to #!/bin/bash"],3,"When run as ./deploy.sh, the shebang chooses the interpreter; /bin/sh may be a POSIX shell such as dash that lacks bash arrays. chmod +x is tempting, but the script already runs (it reaches the syntax error), so the execute bit is not the problem.","Domain 4: Bash scripting",["The script already runs and reaches the syntax error, so it is already executable.","set -e stops on errors but does not add array support to /bin/sh.","The extension is ignored; the shebang still selects /bin/sh.",null],3],["x39",0,4,"A script runs rm $file, and it misbehaves when the filename contains spaces, trying to delete several wrong names. How should the line be written?",["rm \"$file\"","rm $file","rm '$file'","rm $(echo $file)"],0,"Double quotes expand the variable but prevent word splitting, so a name with spaces stays one argument. Single quotes are tempting, but they stop the expansion altogether, so rm looks for a file literally named $file.","Domain 4: Bash scripting",[null,"Unquoted, the value is split on spaces into several arguments, which is the bug.","Single quotes stop expansion, so rm looks for a file literally named $file.","Command substitution without quotes still splits the result on spaces."],2],["x40",0,4,"A service script should accept start, stop or restart as its first argument and print a usage message for anything else. Which bash construct fits this most cleanly?",["A for loop over \"$@\"","A case statement on \"$1\"","A while read loop","An indexed array of arguments"],1,"A case statement matches one value against several patterns, with *) as the catch-all for usage. A for loop is tempting because it handles arguments, but it iterates over all of them rather than choosing an action based on one.","Domain 4: Bash control flow",["A for loop walks over every argument instead of picking one action from $1.",null,"while read reads input lines, not a command-line argument.","An array stores values but does not choose an action by matching patterns."],1],["x41",0,4,"A script creates a temporary file in $tmp. It must be deleted however the script ends, including errors under set -e or Ctrl+C. What should be added near the top?",["set -u to catch unset variables","rm -f \"$tmp\" as the script's last line","trap 'rm -f \"$tmp\"' EXIT","exit 0 as the script's last line"],2,"A trap on EXIT runs the cleanup command whenever the shell exits, including after errors or an interrupt. Putting rm on the last line is tempting, but that line never runs if the script stops early.","Domain 4: Safer scripts",["set -u only catches unset variables; it does not clean up files.","The last line never runs if the script exits early from an error or Ctrl+C.",null,"exit 0 at the end also never runs after an early exit, and it deletes nothing."],3],["x42",0,4,"A script must read hosts.txt one full line at a time, keeping spaces and backslashes in each line exactly as they are. Which loop is correct?",["for line in $(cat hosts.txt); do ...; done","cat hosts.txt | xargs -n1 echo","for line in hosts.txt; do ...; done","while IFS= read -r line; do ...; done < hosts.txt"],3,"while IFS= read -r reads whole lines, keeps leading spaces and does not treat backslashes as escapes. The for over $(cat) is tempting because it is short, but it splits on every space, so a line becomes several words.","Domain 4: Bash control flow",["$(cat) output is split on every space, so each line breaks into separate words.","xargs splits on whitespace and handles quotes and backslashes specially.","This loops once over the literal word hosts.txt, not over the file's lines.",null],3],["x43",0,4,"A Python admin script needs specific library versions, and the server's system Python packages are managed by dnf and must not change. What is the best approach?",["Create a venv and pip install inside it","sudo pip install the libraries system-wide","Copy the libraries into /usr/lib/python3","Install them from a third-party dnf repo"],0,"A virtual environment isolates the script's packages from the system Python, so dnf-managed packages stay untouched. A system-wide sudo pip install is tempting because it is quick, but it can overwrite files that dnf manages and break system tools.","Domain 4: Python basics",[null,"A system-wide pip install can overwrite files that dnf manages and break system tools.","Copying libraries by hand into system paths bypasses package management and can break things.","A third-party repo may not have the exact versions and still changes the system Python."],2],["x44",0,4,"A Python script must store hostnames with their IP addresses and quickly look up an IP by hostname. Which built-in data type fits best?",["A list","A dictionary","A tuple","A set"],1,"A dictionary maps keys (hostnames) to values (IPs) with fast lookup by key. A set is tempting because it also offers fast membership checks, but it stores single values and cannot map a hostname to an IP.","Domain 4: Python basics",["A list would need a slow search to find a hostname and does not map keys to values.",null,"A tuple is an immutable sequence without key-based lookup.","A set stores single values and cannot map a hostname to an IP."],1],["x45",0,4,"A developer wants the commits on their own feature branch, which nobody else uses, replayed on top of the latest main so the history stays linear. Which command should they run from the feature branch?",["git merge main","git revert main","git rebase main","git reset --hard main"],2,"git rebase main reapplies the branch's commits on top of main, producing linear history. git merge main is tempting because it also brings in main's changes, but it creates a merge commit instead of a linear history.","Domain 4: Git",["merge main brings in the changes but adds a merge commit, so history is not linear.","revert creates commits that undo changes; it does not replay the branch on main.",null,"reset --hard main discards the branch's own commits."],2],["x46",0,4,"An Ansible playbook deploys the nginx config from a template. nginx should restart only when the template task actually changes the file. How is this done?",["Add a shell task that always restarts nginx","Set gather_facts: false in the play","Encrypt the template with ansible-vault","Use notify to trigger a restart handler"],3,"A task with notify triggers a handler only when the task reports changed, and handlers run once at the end of the play. Always restarting with a shell task is tempting because it guarantees the new config, but it causes needless restarts and breaks idempotence.","Domain 4: Ansible",["Always restarting causes needless downtime and breaks idempotence.","gather_facts controls fact collection and has nothing to do with restarts.","ansible-vault encrypts secrets; it does not control when services restart.",null],2],["x47",0,4,"A team follows a GitOps workflow for its infrastructure. How should an engineer change a production setting?",["Merge the change in Git and let automation apply it","SSH into each production host and edit the config by hand","Email the change to the ops team to apply manually","Edit the live resources in the cloud console"],0,"In GitOps, Git is the source of truth and automation reconciles the live environment to match it. Editing in the cloud console is tempting because it is fast, but it creates drift that the automation may overwrite and that is not reviewed or tracked.","Domain 4: CI/CD and GitOps",[null,"Manual edits over SSH cause drift and skip the Git review and history.","Emailing changes for manual work bypasses Git as the source of truth.","Console edits create drift that the automation may overwrite and that nobody reviewed."],2],["x48",0,5,"A server is sluggish. vmstat 1 shows the si and so columns consistently high, while the CPU idle percentage is moderate. What does this indicate?",["User processes are saturating every CPU core","Memory is short, so pages are swapping","A disk has developed failing sectors","The NIC is dropping inbound packets"],1,"si and so show pages swapped in and out per second; constant high values mean the system is short of RAM and swapping heavily. CPU saturation is tempting because the server is slow, but moderate idle time rules it out.","Domain 5: Performance",["The CPU still has idle time, so it is not saturated.",null,"Failing sectors show up as I/O errors in dmesg, not in vmstat's swap columns.","Network drops do not appear in vmstat's si and so columns."],3],["x49",0,5,"A user worries because free -h shows very little free memory, but buff/cache is large and the available column is high. What is the correct interpretation?",["The server needs more RAM installed right away","A memory leak is consuming the RAM","Normal: cache is reclaimed when apps need it","Swap has been disabled on the server"],2,"Linux uses spare RAM as page cache and gives it back when applications need memory; the available column shows what apps can still use. A memory leak is tempting, but a leak would show as growing process memory and falling available memory, not a large cache.","Domain 5: Performance",["High available memory means apps still have plenty of RAM, so no urgent upgrade is needed.","A leak would show growing process memory and falling available memory, not a large cache.",null,"free shows the swap line separately; the cache numbers say nothing about swap being off."],3],["x50",0,5,"An admin wants to see exactly what the DNS server 10.0.0.53 returns for app.example.com, without /etc/hosts or other local sources affecting the result. Which command fits?",["ping app.example.com","getent hosts app.example.com","host -l 10.0.0.53","dig @10.0.0.53 app.example.com"],3,"dig @server sends the query straight to that DNS server and shows its answer. getent hosts is tempting because it resolves names, but it follows nsswitch.conf, so /etc/hosts entries can answer first.","Domain 5: Networking",["ping uses the system resolver, including /etc/hosts, and does not show the DNS answer.","getent follows nsswitch.conf, so /etc/hosts entries can answer first.","host -l asks for a zone transfer listing; it does not query this name against that server.",null],2],["x51",0,5,"Clients can't reach an HTTPS service. The admin wants to confirm whether TCP 443 packets are even arriving on eth0. Which command shows this?",["tcpdump -i eth0 tcp port 443","ss -tln sport = :443","nmap -p 443 localhost","ip link show eth0"],0,"tcpdump captures packets on the interface, so it shows whether client traffic to port 443 arrives at all. ss is tempting because it shows the socket listening, but it cannot show whether packets are reaching the interface.","Domain 5: Networking",[null,"ss shows the listening socket but cannot show whether packets reach the interface.","Scanning localhost tests the loopback path, not traffic arriving on eth0.","ip link shows the interface state, not the traffic on port 443."],2],["x52",0,5,"ss on db01 shows PostgreSQL listening on 0.0.0.0:5432, but remote clients time out. From a client, the admin wants to see whether port 5432 looks open, closed or filtered. Which command fits?",["ss -tulpn on the client","nmap -p 5432 db01 from the client","ping db01 from the client","ip route on the server to check its routes"],1,"nmap reports a port as open, closed or filtered from the client's view, and filtered points to a firewall. ping is tempting, but ICMP succeeding does not prove TCP 5432 is reachable.","Domain 5: Networking",["ss on the client shows the client's own sockets, not whether the server port is reachable.",null,"A successful ping proves ICMP works, not that TCP 5432 gets through.","Routes on the server do not show whether a firewall filters the port."],2],["x53",0,5,"Users report intermittent packet loss to a remote site. The admin wants continuously updated loss and latency statistics for every hop on the path. Which tool fits?",["ping -c 4 remote-site","traceroute remote-site","mtr remote-site","dig remote-site"],2,"mtr combines traceroute and ping, keeps probing and shows per-hop loss and latency over time. traceroute is tempting because it shows the hops, but it is a one-time snapshot that easily misses intermittent loss.","Domain 5: Networking",["Four pings to the destination give no per-hop view and may miss intermittent loss.","traceroute is a one-time snapshot that easily misses intermittent loss.",null,"dig tests DNS resolution, not packet loss on the path."],2],["x54",0,5,"An admin needs to boot a server into a single-user maintenance mode that mounts local filesystems but starts no network services. Which unit should be passed with systemd.unit= at the GRUB menu?",["emergency.target","multi-user.target","graphical.target","rescue.target"],3,"rescue.target gives a single-user root shell with local filesystems mounted and basic services running. emergency.target is tempting because it is also for repairs, but it is more minimal, with only the root filesystem mounted read-only.","Domain 5: Boot problems",["emergency.target is more minimal and mounts only the root filesystem, read-only.","multi-user.target starts network services, which is not wanted here.","graphical.target starts a full system with a desktop and network services.",null],3],["x55",0,5,"An mdadm RAID 1 array on a server is reported as degraded. Which command quickly shows the array's state and which member has failed?",["cat /proc/mdstat","lsblk -f","pvs","smartctl -a /dev/md0"],0,"/proc/mdstat shows each md array, its members and markers such as [U_] or (F) for a failed disk. smartctl is tempting because it checks disk health, but it reads SMART data from physical drives, not the md array device.","Domain 5: Hardware and RAID",[null,"lsblk -f shows filesystems and UUIDs, not the RAID array's health.","pvs lists LVM physical volumes, not md array members.","SMART data comes from physical drives, not the md array device."],1],["x56",0,5,"User bob is locked out after too many failed logins. The server uses pam_faillock, and bob's password itself is fine. How should the admin unlock him?",["passwd -u bob","faillock --user bob --reset","usermod -U bob","chage -E -1 bob"],1,"faillock --reset clears bob's failed-attempt record, which is what pam_faillock uses to lock him out. passwd -u is tempting because it unlocks accounts, but it removes a password lock (the ! in /etc/shadow), not the faillock tally.","Domain 5: Security issues",["passwd -u removes a password lock (the ! in /etc/shadow), not the faillock record.",null,"usermod -U also removes only the password lock, not the failed-attempt tally.","chage -E -1 clears an account expiry date, which is not what locked bob."],3],["x57",0,5,"A server's clock is several minutes off, and timedatectl shows NTP service: inactive. Which command turns on automatic time synchronization?",["timedatectl set-timezone UTC","hwclock --hctosys","timedatectl set-ntp true","date -s now"],2,"timedatectl set-ntp true enables the system's NTP sync service so the clock stays correct. hwclock --hctosys is tempting because it sets the system time, but it copies the hardware clock, which may be wrong too, and does not keep syncing.","Domain 5: Time sync",["Changing the time zone does not correct a wrong clock or turn on syncing.","hwclock --hctosys copies the hardware clock, which may also be wrong, and does not keep syncing.",null,"date -s now just sets the time to its current wrong value."],1],["x58",0,5,"apt upgrade keeps reporting nginx as kept back, and apt-mark showhold lists nginx because an admin held it months ago. The team now wants nginx upgraded normally. Which command should be run first?",["apt autoremove nginx","dpkg --configure -a","apt-get clean","apt-mark unhold nginx"],3,"apt-mark unhold removes the hold so apt can upgrade nginx again. dpkg --configure -a is tempting as a common repair command, but it only finishes configuring half-installed packages and does not affect holds.","Domain 5: Package problems",["autoremove removes unused dependencies and does not release a hold.","dpkg --configure -a finishes half-installed packages and does not affect holds.","apt-get clean clears the download cache only.",null],2],["x59",0,5,"dnf install fails with a GPG check error for a package from a newly added vendor repository. What is the correct fix?",["Import the vendor's signing key after verifying it","Set gpgcheck=0 in the vendor's .repo file and retry","Run dnf clean all, then retry the install","Install the package with rpm --nosignature"],0,"dnf needs the vendor's public key to verify package signatures; import it from a trusted source after checking its fingerprint. Setting gpgcheck=0 is tempting because the install then works, but it turns off integrity checking for every package from that repo.","Domain 5: Package problems",[null,"gpgcheck=0 turns off signature checks for every package from that repo.","Clearing the cache does not add the missing signing key.","Skipping signature checks installs unverified packages, which defeats their purpose."],3],["x60",0,5,"podman run registry.example.com/team/api:2.4 fails with a 'manifest unknown' error, while pulling api:2.3 from the same registry works. What is the most likely cause?",["The host has no free disk space left","The image tag was not found in the registry","The container's port is already in use","SELinux is blocking the container's volume mount"],1,"manifest unknown means the registry has no image manifest for that name and tag, so the 2.4 tag likely does not exist. A port conflict is tempting because it is a common podman error, but it happens when the container starts, after the image is already pulled.","Domain 5: Container problems",["Low disk space causes storage write errors, not a manifest unknown response from the registry.",null,"A port conflict happens when the container starts, after the image is already pulled.","A volume mount denial happens at run time and would not stop the 2.4 image from being found."],2]]);
